"""English — details, prophets_c_2 (50 questions)."""

D = {
"prophets_201": "Surah Sad tells the Prophet ﷺ to remember Dawud, \"possessor of strength\", who constantly turned back to his Lord (38:17), and then describes his companions in praise: \"Indeed, We subjected the mountains to exalt with him in the evening and after sunrise, and the birds, assembled, all turning to Him with him\" (38:18-19). Mountains and birds, which do not speak our language, joined a prophet in remembering Allah at both ends of the day. A Muslim takes from this that glorifying Allah morning and evening places a person in the company of all creation.",
"prophets_202": "The verse follows the mountains and birds that glorified with Dawud: \"And We strengthened his kingdom and gave him wisdom and decisive speech\" (38:20). The next verses show that speech in use, when two disputants climbed over the wall of his prayer chamber and asked him to judge between them justly (38:21-22). A firm kingdom, wisdom and clear words to settle a matter are named together as gifts from Allah, not achievements of a king. A Muslim learns that authority is only sound when joined to wisdom and just judgement.",
"prophets_203": "Surah Saba first lists what Allah gave Dawud: the mountains and birds repeating praise with him, and iron made soft for him (34:10). The command then follows: \"Make full coats of mail and measure precisely the links, and work righteousness; indeed I am Seeing of what you do\" (34:11). The verse pairs careful craftsmanship with righteous conduct, and closes by reminding Dawud that Allah watches the work. A Muslim takes from it that doing a task well, down to its smallest measure, is itself part of worship.",
"prophets_204": "The verse opens the story of Sulaiman in Surah An-Naml: \"And We had given to Dawud and Sulaiman knowledge, and they said: Praise be to Allah, who has favoured us over many of His believing servants\" (27:15). The next verse tells that Sulaiman inherited Dawud and announced that he had been taught the speech of birds and given from all things, calling it clear bounty (27:16). Father and son received knowledge and answered it with praise. A Muslim learns to meet every gift of understanding with thanks to the One who gave it.",
"prophets_205": "The scene is the valley of the ants, where an ant cried: \"O ants, enter your dwellings, lest Sulaiman and his soldiers crush you while they perceive not\" (27:18). \"So he smiled, amused at her speech, and said: My Lord, enable me to be grateful for Your favour which You have bestowed upon me and upon my parents, and to do righteousness of which You approve\" (27:19). A king turned a tiny creature's warning into a prayer of thanks. A Muslim learns to answer every reminder of Allah's favour with gratitude rather than pride.",
"prophets_206": "Sulaiman had noticed the hoopoe missing and promised it a severe punishment unless it brought a clear reason (27:20-21). The bird returned and said: \"I have come to you from Saba with certain news. I found a woman ruling them, and she has been given of all things, and she has a great throne\" (27:22-23). It added that she and her people prostrated to the sun (27:24). Sulaiman replied that he would test whether the report was true (27:27). A Muslim learns that news is worth acting on only once it has been verified.",
"prophets_207": "Before this, her own throne had been brought to Sulaiman, and she was asked whether hers was like it (27:42). Then \"she was told: Enter the palace. But when she saw it, she thought it was a body of water and uncovered her shins. He said: Indeed, it is a palace made smooth with glass\" (27:44). In that same verse she answered: \"My Lord, indeed I have wronged myself, and I submit with Sulaiman to Allah, Lord of the worlds.\" A Muslim takes from this that recognising one's error and turning to Allah is the true purpose of every wonder.",
"prophets_208": "\"And gathered for Sulaiman were his soldiers of the jinn and men and birds, and they were set in rows\" (27:17). This follows his words that he had been taught the speech of birds and given from all things (27:16), and it leads straight into the valley of the ants (27:18). Three kinds of creatures marched under one prophet-king, in order, because Allah had placed them under his command. A Muslim learns that Sulaiman's power came from Allah, who presents it as a favour calling for thanks (27:19).",
"prophets_209": "Surah Maryam opens with \"a mention of the mercy of your Lord to His servant Zakariyya, when he called to his Lord a private call\" (19:2-3). He said: \"My Lord, indeed my bones have weakened, and my head has filled with white, and never have I been in my supplication to You, my Lord, unblessed\" (19:4). He then asked for an heir, because his wife was barren and he feared for those after him (19:5-6). The answer was good tidings of a boy named Yahya (19:7). A Muslim learns to bring weakness and old age before Allah in quiet, trusting prayer.",
"prophets_210": "Zakariyya had prayed in the chamber for good offspring (3:38), and the angels called to him while he stood praying with the good tidings of Yahya (3:39). When he asked how he could have a son in old age, he was told: \"Your sign is that you will not speak to the people for three days except by gesture. And remember your Lord much and exalt Him with praise in the evening and the morning\" (3:41). The sign was silence toward people, joined to constant remembrance of Allah. A Muslim takes from it that a gift from Allah is met with more remembrance, not less.",
"prophets_211": "This is the first thing the Qur'an says about Yahya after his birth: \"O Yahya, take the Scripture with determination. And We gave him judgement while yet a boy\" (19:12). The verses that follow describe him: tenderness from Allah and purity, fear of Allah, and dutifulness to his parents (19:13-14). Then comes a greeting: \"And peace be upon him the day he was born and the day he dies and the day he is raised alive\" (19:15). A Muslim learns that holding firmly to Allah's Book is expected even of the young, and grows into a whole character.",
"prophets_212": "Just before this, the angels said: \"O Maryam, indeed Allah has chosen you and purified you and chosen you above the women of the worlds\" (3:42). Then came the command: \"O Maryam, be devoutly obedient to your Lord and prostrate and bow with those who bow\" (3:43). Her upbringing had prepared her for it: Allah accepted her with good acceptance, caused her to grow well, and put her in the care of Zakariyya (3:37). Being chosen was answered with more worship, not pride. A Muslim learns that honour from Allah is carried by humility and prayer.",
"prophets_213": "The pains of childbirth drove Maryam to the trunk of a palm tree, and she wished she had died and been forgotten (19:23). Then a voice called from below her: \"Do not grieve; your Lord has provided beneath you a stream. And shake toward you the trunk of the palm tree; it will drop upon you ripe, fresh dates. So eat and drink and be contented\" (19:24-26). The stream and the dates were given, yet she was still told to shake the trunk herself. A Muslim learns that Allah's provision is near, and that a person still makes the effort asked of them.",
"prophets_214": "When Maryam pointed to the child, her people asked how they could speak to one in the cradle (19:29). He answered: \"Indeed, I am the servant of Allah. He has given me the Scripture and made me a prophet\" (19:30), then: \"And He has made me blessed wherever I am, and has enjoined upon me prayer and zakat as long as I remain alive\" (19:31), and dutiful to his mother (19:32). His first words named himself a servant of Allah, and his duties are the prayer and zakat asked of every believer. A Muslim learns that prayer and zakat bind a person for life, as they bound 'Isa.",
"prophets_215": "The verse answers people who laughed when 'Isa was mentioned as an example and asked whether their gods were better than he (43:57-58). \"He was not but a servant upon whom We bestowed favour, and We made him an example for the Children of Israel\" (43:59). Later in the surah 'Isa himself says: \"Indeed, Allah is my Lord and your Lord, so worship Him; this is a straight path\" (43:64). Both verses say the same: a favoured servant who called people to worship Allah alone. A Muslim honours 'Isa as a prophet and servant, and worships only the Lord he worshipped.",
"prophets_216": "On the Day of Resurrection, Allah will ask 'Isa whether he told people to take him and his mother as gods besides Allah (5:116). He will answer: \"Exalted are You! It was not for me to say that to which I have no right. If I had said it, You would have known it. You know what is within myself, and I do not know what is within Yourself\" (5:116). He will add that he told them only what he was commanded: \"Worship Allah, my Lord and your Lord\" (5:117). A Muslim learns from 'Isa's own words that he called to the worship of Allah alone.",
"prophets_217": "The verse addresses the People of the Scripture, telling them not to commit excess in their religion (4:171). It then states who 'Isa is: \"The Messiah, 'Isa son of Maryam, was but a messenger of Allah and His word which He directed to Maryam and a soul from Him. So believe in Allah and His messengers, and do not say Three; desist, it is better for you\" (4:171). The next verse adds that the Messiah would never disdain to be a servant of Allah (4:172). A Muslim learns to speak of 'Isa exactly as the Qur'an does: messenger, word and spirit from Allah.",
"prophets_218": "Surah Ad-Duha first assures the Prophet ﷺ: \"Your Lord has not taken leave of you, nor has He detested you\" (93:3). Then come the three favours: \"Did He not find you an orphan and give you refuge? And He found you lost and guided you. And He found you poor and made you self-sufficient\" (93:6-8). The surah closes by turning each favour into a duty: do not oppress the orphan, do not repel the one who asks, and speak of the favour of your Lord (93:9-11). A Muslim learns to remember past blessings and to pass them on to others.",
"prophets_219": "Surah Ash-Sharh first recalls three gifts to the Prophet ﷺ: the opening of his breast, the lifting of his burden and the raising of his name (94:1-4). Then comes the promise, twice: \"For indeed, with hardship comes ease. Indeed, with hardship comes ease\" (94:5-6). The surah ends with the answer expected of him: \"So when you have finished, then stand up, and to your Lord direct your longing\" (94:7-8). The words say \"with\" hardship, not \"after\" it: ease is already beside the difficulty. A Muslim learns to hold on in hard times, trusting that ease is near.",
"prophets_220": "The surah has three verses: \"Indeed, We have granted you al-Kawthar. So pray to your Lord and sacrifice. Indeed, your enemy is the one cut off\" (108:1-3). It begins with a gift given, moves to the two acts asked in return, prayer and sacrifice, and ends by turning the insult of the enemy back on him. The name al-Kawthar comes from a root meaning abundance, and the verse presents it as already granted. A Muslim learns that the right answer to a gift from Allah is worship directed to Him alone.",
"prophets_221": "These are the last verses of Surah At-Tawbah: \"There has certainly come to you a Messenger from among yourselves. Grievous to him is what you suffer; he is concerned over you, and to the believers is kind and merciful\" (9:128). The next verse tells him what to say if people turn away: \"Sufficient for me is Allah; there is no deity except Him. On Him I have relied\" (9:129). The Messenger ﷺ is described as one of the people, pained by their pain, and resting on Allah alone. A Muslim learns to care for others as the Prophet ﷺ cared.",
"prophets_222": "The verse belongs to the passage of Surah Al-'Imran that reviews the battle of Uhud (3:152-155). \"So by mercy from Allah you were lenient with them. And if you had been rude and harsh in heart, they would have disbanded from about you. So pardon them and ask forgiveness for them and consult them in the matter\" (3:159). His gentleness is named a mercy from Allah, and it is followed by pardon, prayer and consultation, even after a defeat. A Muslim learns that softness of heart holds people together where harshness scatters them.",
"prophets_223": "The verse stands in the account of the Confederates, when armies came and \"eyes shifted and hearts reached the throats\" (33:10). \"There has certainly been for you in the Messenger of Allah an excellent pattern for anyone whose hope is in Allah and the Last Day and who remembers Allah often\" (33:21). The next verse shows the believers seeing the enemy and saying this was what Allah and His Messenger had promised, which only increased their faith (33:22). The example is set at the moment of greatest fear. A Muslim learns to look to the Prophet ﷺ for how to act in every situation, including hardship.",
"prophets_224": "\"And We have not sent you except comprehensively to mankind, as a bringer of good tidings and a warner, but most of the people do not know\" (34:28). The verse follows the story of Saba (34:15-19) and answers those who asked when the promise would come (34:29). Elsewhere the Prophet ﷺ is told to say: \"O mankind, indeed I am the Messenger of Allah to you all\" (7:158), and he is called \"a mercy to the worlds\" (21:107). His message is not for one tribe or one land. A Muslim learns that the call of Muhammad ﷺ reaches every person.",
"prophets_225": "\"And say: Truth has come and falsehood has departed. Indeed, falsehood is bound to depart\" (17:81). Just before it, the Prophet ﷺ is taught to pray for a sound entrance, a sound exit and a supporting authority from Allah (17:80), and right after it the Qur'an is called \"healing and mercy for the believers\" (17:82). The sira records him reciting the verse at the conquest of Makkah as the idols around the Ka'bah fell, the words fulfilled before his eyes. A Muslim repeats the verse as a certainty: whatever stands against the truth has no lasting hold.",
"prophets_226": "The verse, one of the last two of Surah Al-Baqarah, describes the faith of the Messenger ﷺ and the believers: \"All of them have believed in Allah and His angels and His books and His messengers, saying: We make no distinction between any of His messengers. And they say: We hear and we obey. Your forgiveness, our Lord, and to You is the destination\" (2:285). The verse that follows is the prayer \"Our Lord, do not impose blame upon us if we forget or err\" (2:286). Belief in every messenger stands beside obedience and the plea for forgiveness. A Muslim believes in all the prophets, without leaving one out.",
"prophets_227": "\"And We certainly sent into every nation a messenger, saying: Worship Allah and avoid taghut. And among them were those whom Allah guided, and among them were those upon whom error was decreed. So travel through the earth and observe how was the end of the deniers\" (16:36). No nation was left without a messenger, and every messenger opened with the same call. The verse then divides the listeners into those who accepted guidance and those who refused, and points to the ruins of the refusers. A Muslim learns that the religion of all the prophets is one: worship Allah alone.",
"prophets_228": "\"And We did not send any messenger except in the language of his people, to make things clear for them\" (14:4). The surah has just described the Book sent down \"that you might bring mankind out of darknesses into the light\" (14:1), and the next verse recalls Musa sent with the same charge to his people (14:5). A message meant to be understood is delivered in words the hearers already know. A Muslim learns that Allah's guidance is meant to be understood, not merely heard.",
"prophets_229": "\"And We sent not before you any messenger except that We revealed to him that there is no deity except Me, so worship Me\" (21:25). The verse comes in a passage where those who take gods besides Allah are told, \"Produce your proof\" (21:24), and it is followed by a rejection of the claim that the Most Merciful has taken a son: \"Rather, they are honoured servants\" (21:26). Every revelation before the Qur'an carried the same first sentence. A Muslim learns that the testimony \"there is no god but Allah\" is the oldest message on earth.",
"prophets_230": "The Qur'an names Ibrahim's line: \"And We gave to him Ishaq and Ya'qub; all of them We guided. And Nuh We guided before, and among his descendants Dawud and Sulaiman and Ayyub and Yusuf and Musa and Harun\" (6:84). The list continues with Zakariyya, Yahya, 'Isa and Ilyas, then Isma'il, Alyasa', Yunus and Lut (6:85-86): seventeen prophets in three verses. The passage then says: \"Those are the ones whom Allah has guided, so from their guidance take an example\" (6:90). A Muslim learns their names as one family of guidance to follow.",
"prophets_231": "\"Those messengers, some of them We caused to exceed others. Among them were those to whom Allah spoke, and He raised some of them in degree. And We gave 'Isa, son of Maryam, clear proofs and supported him with the Pure Spirit\" (2:253). The verse sits beside the believers' own words at the end of the same surah, \"We make no distinction between any of His messengers\" (2:285). The two fit together: Allah ranks His messengers, while the believer honours every one of them. A Muslim believes in all the messengers and leaves their ranking to Allah.",
"prophets_232": "\"And when Allah took the covenant of the prophets: Whatever I give you of the Scripture and wisdom, and then there comes to you a messenger confirming what is with you, you must believe in him and support him. He said: Have you acknowledged and taken upon that My commitment? They said: We have acknowledged it\" (3:81). The next verse adds that whoever turns away after this is defiantly disobedient (3:82). The prophets themselves pledged to accept the messenger who would confirm their books. A Muslim learns that following Muhammad ﷺ fulfils what every earlier prophet promised.",
"prophets_233": "\"He has ordained for you of religion what He enjoined upon Nuh, and that which We have revealed to you, and what We enjoined upon Ibrahim and Musa and 'Isa: establish the religion and do not be divided therein\" (42:13). Five names stand in the verse: four earlier prophets and the Prophet ﷺ himself, addressed as \"you\". What was given to all of them is described as one religion, with one instruction attached: keep it standing and do not split over it. A Muslim learns that Islam is the religion of Nuh, Ibrahim, Musa and 'Isa, not a new invention.",
"prophets_234": "\"And We have already sent messengers before you. Among them are those whose story We have related to you, and among them are those whose story We have not related to you. And it was not for any messenger to bring a sign except by permission of Allah\" (40:78). The same statement appears in Surah An-Nisa, where after listing messengers Allah adds \"and messengers about whom We have not related to you\" (4:164). The Qur'an names some prophets and is silent about the rest. A Muslim believes in every messenger Allah sent, named or unnamed, without inventing stories for the rest.",
"prophets_235": "\"And We sent not before you except men to whom We revealed, from among the people of the cities. So have they not travelled through the earth and observed how was the end of those before them?\" (12:109). The verse comes at the close of Surah Yusuf, after Yusuf's long story, and just before the surah says that in the stories of the messengers \"there was a lesson for those of understanding\" (12:111). Messengers were men, living in towns, speaking to their neighbours. A Muslim learns that the prophets were human beings whose lives can be followed.",
"prophets_236": "\"And certainly were messengers denied before you, but they were patient over the denial and were harmed until Our victory came to them. And none can alter the words of Allah. And there has certainly come to you some information about the messengers\" (6:34). The verse before it speaks to the Prophet ﷺ directly: \"We know that you are saddened by what they say\" (6:33). The comfort offered is the record of the earlier messengers: denial, patience, harm, then help that never failed. A Muslim learns that patience under rejection is the way of every prophet, and that Allah's help follows it.",
"prophets_237": "\"Indeed, Allah chose Adam and Nuh and the family of Ibrahim and the family of 'Imran over the worlds, descendants, some of them from others\" (3:33-34). The verse opens the passage that tells how the wife of 'Imran vowed the child in her womb to Allah's service and gave birth to Maryam (3:35-36), followed by the stories of Zakariyya and 'Isa. The chosen families are linked as one line of descent, from Adam down to the house of 'Imran. A Muslim learns that the prophets belong to a single chain of chosen people, and honours the family of Maryam within it.",
"prophets_238": "\"And when Musa said to his people: O my people, remember the favour of Allah upon you when He appointed among you prophets and made you kings. O my people, enter the Holy Land which Allah has assigned to you, and do not turn back and become losers\" (5:20-21). The people answered that a mighty folk lived there, and finally said: \"Go, you and your Lord, and fight; indeed, we are remaining right here\" (5:22-24). The land was then forbidden to them for forty years (5:26). A Muslim learns that remembering Allah's favours is meant to give courage to obey Him.",
"prophets_239": "The verse describes those who follow \"the Messenger, the unlettered prophet, whom they find written in what they have of the Torah and the Gospel, who enjoins upon them what is right and forbids them what is wrong, and relieves them of their burden and the shackles which were upon them\" (7:157). It comes as Allah answers Musa's prayer for his people, promising His mercy to those who fear Him and follow this prophet (7:156). It closes by calling successful those who believe in him, honour him, support him and follow his light (7:157). A Muslim learns that the earlier scriptures pointed ahead to Muhammad ﷺ.",
"prophets_240": "Nuh said to his people: \"O my people, if my residence and my reminding of the signs of Allah has become burdensome upon you, then upon Allah I have relied. So resolve upon your plan, you and your associates; then carry it out upon me and give me no respite\" (10:71). He added that he asked no payment from them: \"My reward is only from Allah, and I have been commanded to be of the Muslims\" (10:72). They denied him, and Allah saved him and those with him in the ship (10:73). A Muslim learns that whoever relies on Allah can face any plot without fear.",
"prophets_241": "Ibrahim spoke these words after asking his father and his people what they worshipped, and declaring their idols his enemies, \"except the Lord of the worlds\" (26:70-77). He then described that Lord: \"Who created me, and He guides me; and it is He who feeds me and gives me drink; and when I am ill, it is He who cures me; and who will cause me to die and then bring me to life\" (26:78-81). He ends by hoping for forgiveness on the Day of Recompense (26:82). Creation, food, healing, death and resurrection are all placed in one pair of hands. A Muslim learns to seek healing from Allah first.",
"prophets_242": "Ibrahim had prayed for a righteous child and been given a forbearing boy (37:100-101). When the boy had grown, Ibrahim told him of his dream that he must sacrifice him, and the son answered: \"O my father, do as you are commanded; you will find me, if Allah wills, of the steadfast\" (37:102). When both had submitted and Ibrahim laid him down on his forehead, Allah called: \"O Ibrahim, you have fulfilled the vision\" (37:103-105), and then: \"And We ransomed him with a great sacrifice\" (37:107). A Muslim learns that when father and son surrendered fully, Allah provided the way out.",
"prophets_243": "\"Say: We have believed in Allah and what has been revealed to us and what has been revealed to Ibrahim and Isma'il and Ishaq and Ya'qub and the Descendants, and what was given to Musa and 'Isa, and what was given to the prophets from their Lord. We make no distinction between any of them, and we are Muslims in submission to Him\" (2:136). The verse answers those who said, \"Be Jews or Christians so you will be guided,\" with the reply \"Rather, the religion of Ibrahim\" (2:135). A Muslim's faith is spelled out here as one declaration covering every prophet.",
"prophets_244": "\"And each story We relate to you from the news of the messengers is that by which We make firm your heart. And there has come to you in this the truth and an instruction and a reminder for the believers\" (11:120). The verse comes near the end of Surah Hud, which has told of Nuh, Hud, Salih, Ibrahim, Lut, Shu'ayb and Musa, each facing a people who rejected him. The stories are given a purpose: to steady the Prophet ﷺ, and to instruct and remind those who believe. A Muslim reads the lives of the prophets as strength for the heart in hard times.",
"prophets_245": "This is the last verse of Surah Al-Kahf: \"Say: I am only a man like you, to whom it has been revealed that your God is one God. So whoever hopes for the meeting with his Lord, let him do righteous work and not associate in the worship of his Lord anyone\" (18:110). The verse before it says that if the sea were ink for the words of Allah, it would run dry before those words ended (18:109). After the surah's long stories, the closing word is the Prophet's ﷺ humanity and the oneness of Allah. A Muslim learns that a prophet is a man who receives revelation, and that pure worship is what Allah asks.",
"prophets_246": "This is the last verse of Surah Al-Fath: \"Muhammad is the Messenger of Allah, and those with him are forceful against the disbelievers, merciful among themselves. You see them bowing and prostrating, seeking bounty from Allah and His pleasure. Their mark is on their faces from the trace of prostration\" (48:29). The same verse says this is their description in the Torah, and that in the Gospel they are like a crop that sends out its shoot, grows firm and stands on its stalk. Firmness and mercy are joined, and both flow from bowing to Allah. A Muslim learns that the companions' strength grew from their worship.",
"prophets_247": "Surah An-Najm opens with an oath by the star, then says: \"Your companion has not strayed, nor has he erred, nor does he speak from his own inclination. It is not but a revelation revealed, taught to him by one intense in strength\" (53:2-5). The Prophet ﷺ is called \"your companion\", the man his people had known, and what he speaks is declared to be revelation, not his own wish. The surah then describes the teacher approaching and the Prophet's ﷺ heart not lying about what it saw (53:6-11). A Muslim receives the Prophet's ﷺ words as revelation, because the Qur'an itself says they are.",
"prophets_248": "The surah has three verses: \"When the victory of Allah has come and the conquest, and you see the people entering into the religion of Allah in multitudes, then exalt with praise of your Lord and ask forgiveness of Him. Indeed, He is ever Accepting of repentance\" (110:1-3). It is a whole surah about how to receive success: not with celebration of oneself, but with glorifying Allah and seeking His pardon. The crowds entering the religion are the sign to look for, and the response is turned entirely toward Allah. A Muslim learns to answer every victory with praise and istighfar.",
"prophets_249": "\"It is He who has sent among the unlettered a Messenger from themselves, reciting to them His verses and purifying them and teaching them the Book and wisdom, although they were before in clear error\" (62:2). The next verse extends the same Messenger to \"others of them who have not yet joined them\" (62:3). The same tasks appear in the prayer Ibrahim made: \"Our Lord, and send among them a messenger from themselves who will recite to them Your verses and teach them the Book and wisdom and purify them\" (2:129). A Muslim learns that Muhammad ﷺ is the answer to Ibrahim's prayer.",
"prophets_250": "\"But Allah would not punish them while you are among them, and Allah would not punish them while they seek forgiveness\" (8:33). The verse before it records what the deniers had said: \"O Allah, if this should be the truth from You, then rain down upon us stones from the sky or bring us a painful punishment\" (8:32). The answer names two shields that held the punishment back: the presence of the Prophet ﷺ among them, and the seeking of forgiveness. A Muslim learns that asking Allah's forgiveness is a protection for a whole community, not only for one person.",
}
